<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usedom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Usedom is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> at the mouth of the Oder, divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, whose part includes the port of Świnoujście. In 1128 an assembly of Pomeranian nobles at Usedom accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a>. In 1630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> landed on the island to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and Sweden held it from 1648 to 1720.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usedom.jpg" alt="Usedom" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeppelubil / Th. Haft, from Torgau / Elbe, Germany · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StrandD%C3%BCne.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
